        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: ANDREW T. TUTT, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner. AMY M. SAHARIA, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument first this morning in Case 22-210, Dupree versus Younger. Mr. Tutt."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a district court resolves a purely legal issue against a party at summary judgment, that issue is preserved for appellate review. There is no requirement that if the case then progresses to a jury trial, the aggrieved party must make two additional motions repeating the same legal argument simply to ensure the issue remains live for review on appeal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That follows from the final judgment rule, the history of appellate review, the history of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, and common sense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In his brief in opposition to certiorari, Mr. Younger suggested an entirely superfluous rule, that parties could preserve purely legal issues by just adding one sentence to Rule 50 JMOL motions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Tutt, how would you define \"purely legal\"? If you were talking about whether or not this was a -- a -- a cause of action or whether a defense was cognizable, I would understand your argument, I think, more clearly. But how would you demonstrate -- how would you prove, for example, exhaustion? It seems that you would need some facts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BARRETT",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why should we decide any of that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What do we say to the -- the JMOL thing doesn't really fit here because there was no affirmative defense presented, so there's no JMOL."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. SAHARIA",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a court denies summary judgment even on a question of law, it delays final adjudication of a claim or defense until trial. The claim or defense remains live. The only way to finally adjudicate a claim or defense at summary judgment is to grant summary judgment to the moving or to the non-moving party."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. SAHARIA",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When a court denies a defendant's motion for summary judgment on an affirmative defense, as here, the defendant must raise his defense at trial to preserve it. And to preserve an argument for entry of a judgment different from the jury's verdict, a defendant must move under Rules 50(a) and (b)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SOTOMAYOR",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But why -- why does that that matter? Because, on appeal, they can't raise that. They're stuck with the record they created."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SAHARIA",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What harm that does to us is that if Petitioner had raised this defense at trial, we would have asked the district court to put on our evidence with respect to the issues of thwarting and opacity, which are highly fact-bound."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SAHARIA",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "12(b)(6) motions deal with the sufficiency of the pleadings. And by the time a case has gone to trial, the evidence overtakes those pleadings."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SAHARIA",
      "case_id": "2022-22-210",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "71ab8cb112e99c01527b75d950776b833dfac95986abbd891dd054d045712125",
      "evidence_id": "ev-71ab8cb112e9-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think it's close to a pure legal question, but I -- I -- I -- I --"
    },
    {
